#!/usr/bin/python3
#
# SYNOPSIS: dir replies and dir updates that are large or at the size limit of
#           the transport message (library test, not `ldmsd`).
#
# A dir request (ldms_xprt_dir()) gets a dir reply that is chunked in records.
# Each record has a JSON string with one entry per set (set metadata and set
# info) and holds as many entries as fit in the maximum message length of the
# transport (Xprt.msg_max). With flags != 0 the client is also sent dir
# updates (DIR_ADD, DIR_UPD), one set per message, until it cancels.
#
# Scheme:
# - node-1 runs an interactive Python server (python/ldms_dir_server.py) that
#   publishes sets, and changes their set info to give exact entry lengths.
# - node-2 runs an interactive Python client (python/ldms_dir_client.py).
# - All sizes are computed from msg_max (see the constants below); they are
#   not measured. The entry at the limit being listed and one byte more being
#   refused (assertions 10 and 11) also checks those constants.
#
# Tests (the assertion numbers are the ones in the output):
# - 4-9    record boundary: entries total limit - 1, limit and limit + 1,
#          with and without more sets after them.
# - 10-15  oversized entry: a set whose entry is larger than an empty record.
#          The server sends an empty record. Also with normal sets around it.
# - 16-18  dir update far over the message limit is dropped; the next ones
#          are delivered.
# - 19-24  dir update boundary: the entry at limit - 1, limit and limit + 1,
#          for DIR_ADD and DIR_UPD.
# - 25     5,000 sets: the records are the ones the packing rule predicts.
# - 26-29  no published set; flags=0 vs flags != 0.
# - 30-32  set name and schema name length limit (LDMS_NAME_MAX is 255 and
#          includes the end of string, so the longest name has 254 characters).
# - 33     the last set is unpublished and published again during dir requests.
# - 34-35  a client process is killed during a burst of dir updates.
# - 36-37  the client closes the transport during a multi-record dir reply.
# - 38-42  sets the client may not read (permission 0600, owned by root; the
#          client runs as the munge user with munge authentication): first,
#          next to a full record, last, and all of them.
#
# They guard the fixes of the dir code in ldms_xprt.c: d621958 (wrong size for
# the end of the JSON string), 59323c7 (empty reply, oversized entry loop,
# last set removed, deadlock when a send fails), bc89e1a and ecb92d5 (client
# side checks of the reply), and dd19698 (name length limits).

