# The dir reply is chunked in records. A record holds the sets that fit in
# the largest message of the transport (Xprt.msg_max), and a set whose entry
# does not fit starts the next record (more=1 on every record but the last).
#
# All sizes come from msg_max. In process_dir_request() (ldms_xprt.c):
#   - a record is a reply header (HDR bytes) and a JSON string of
#     msg_max - HDR bytes;
#   - the JSON string starts with PREFIX bytes ({ "directory" : [) and keeps
#     RESERVE bytes at the end for "]}" and the end of string;
#   - so the entries in a record total at most
#         LIMIT = msg_max - HDR - PREFIX - RESERVE
# In send_dir_update() a dir update has one entry and is dropped when
#         HDR + UPD_SYNTAX + entry + 1 >= msg_max
# so its entry is at most
#         LIMIT_U = msg_max - HDR - UPD_SYNTAX - 2
# The server helper entry_len() gives the length of an entry (it follows the
# format of the entry in __ldms_format_set_meta_as_json()), and fit() / the
# `entry` argument of add_set() make an entry of an exact length.
# The assertions 10 and 11 (an entry of LIMIT is listed, LIMIT + 1 is not) check
# these numbers and entry_len() against the server.

HDR = 32          # struct ldms_reply_hdr (20) + struct ldms_dir_reply (12)
PREFIX = 17       # len('{ "directory" : [')
RESERVE = 4       # "]}" + end of string, with the -3 of the entry check
UPD_SYNTAX = 21   # len('{ "directory" : [  ]}')

MSG_MAX = int(pycmd(cli, "c.x.msg_max"))
